WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.150
Better Call Saul is one of the 
best shows on TV right now.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.780
It's as entertaining as it is inventive.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.360
It feels distinct

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.840
even in the so-called Golden Age of TV.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.880
When I first heard that Vince Gilligan and the crew

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.420
were making a Breaking Bad spinoff centered around a unique but goofy side character

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.900
I'll admit I was skeptical

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:24.200
but Better Call Saul has turned out to be something wonderful and amazing in its own right.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.460
And as the third season wraps up

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.720
I want to talk about why I think it's such a special show

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.200
examining specifically the aspects of the show that

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.240
set it apart from most shows on television right now.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.620
This video is free of *major* plot spoilers

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.260
but I'll be using examples from Seasons 1 &amp; 2.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.520
[theme music]

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.800
I want to start with a quick example of the kind of visual storytelling that

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.040
makes me love Better Call Saul.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.020
In the pilot of the show Jimmy McGill our main character

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.220
is standing at an elevator and we have no idea where

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.469
and we get this wide shot with a 
dented trashcan in the foreground.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.954
Jimmy Looks at it so we know it's important

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.940
but we don't understand the significance of it yet.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.140
The scene progresses and we find ourselves in this huge fancy law firm

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.380
where Jimmy seems to know everybody.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.300
They could have introduced this location 
with an establishing shot

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.460
of Jimmy entering through the front door

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.080
but by introducing the scene with Jimmy coming up through the basement on the elevator

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.980
we've already contextually started to associate frustration

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.240
and possibly anger with this location

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.840
even though Jimmy seems friendly with everyone.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:54.740
As the scene continues, we get a few minutes of dialogue that gives us background about where we are and Jimmy's relationship to these people

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.460
HOWARD: That's money for Chuck

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.000
Isn't that what you wanted?

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.400
JIMMY: A measly 26 grand?

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:05.620
Another short scene updates us on a subplot from the episode without using any dialogue

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:07.760
and then five minutes later

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.280
just when we started to forget about it:

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.120
[kicking trashcan]

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:16.840
pay off

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.860
[denting of trashcan intensifies]

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:22.960
[sad steel guitar]

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.520
With a brilliant shot that pulls back

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.920
as an amazing reveal

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.060
And with just a few more shots they establish the entire context

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:36.500
of a relationship

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.620
We learn so much about these two characters' relationship and history visually

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.220
that they can say almost nothing to each other.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.000
JIMMY: Couldn't you just -
KIM: No I can't.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.900
And we already understand so much.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.220
This whole sequence uses visuals to establish the fact that Jimmy has a history at this place

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.740
He's worked with these people and he's experienced frustrations here in the past

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.740
but not a word of exposition is needed.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.180
Probably the most immediately noticeable thing about Better Call Saul

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.940
is the uniqueness of its look.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.400
I could spend an entire video talking about the use of wide shots in this show

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.880
building upon some of the established visual themes in Breaking Bad.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.480
Wide shots are used to an amazing effect.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.220
In most shows and even in many movies these days

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.180
wide shots often serve simply as an establishing shot

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.540
but Better Call Saul probably more than any other show I've seen

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.500
uses them to their full potential.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.600
In addition to establishing places and settings

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.400
super wide shots communicate a sense of aloneness

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.840
and they're used for dramatic emphasis

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.540
much like a close-up is used in many shows.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.720
Randomly grab an episode of any network crime show or law show off the shelf

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.800
and you often end up with scenes that consist of

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.640
a wide establishing shot or two or three people talking

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.780
followed by a series of over-the-shoulder

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:09.960
medium close-ups.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.440
Better Call Saul a show where many of the characters

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.540
are lawyers and paralegals could easily fall into this trap

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.980
but they keep the scenes grounded in the world

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.940
with these wide and ultra wide shots.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.000
But the use of wide angles - while the most obvious -

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.840
isn't the extent of what makes the cinematography special.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.340
They'll often use longer takes,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:33.740
dynamic blocking

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.060
and go the extra mile with location work

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.120
to create some incredibly cinematic moments.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.320
Better Call Saul has a slow deliberate pacing that's rarely found on TV.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:03.240
The very first post-title scene of the show is a quiet awkward courtroom.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.920
The cold open before the title had almost no dialogue

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:08.720
and that's followed up with this:

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.800
[awkward slurping]

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.320
It's eight minutes from the start of the show until you get a real

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.380
audible line of dialogue said by a character on the screen.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.200
This is *usually* the time when pilot episodes are trying to suck you in

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:32.620
with something exciting or intense.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.480
Even Breaking Bad started off with more of a bang.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:42.040
But Better Call Saul establishes itself from the start

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:45.960
as being more interested in the quieter slower workings

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.200
of the characters and the world.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:55.520
The dramas that are happening within each episode are small compared to those in many other shows

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.640
or even compared to Breaking Bad.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.540
Better Call Saul does an excellent job of unfolding those smaller dramas in a way that feels meaningful.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.140
A smaller story isn't less worthy of the screen

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.980
it just needs a different lens to view it by.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.240
Better Call Saul gives us that lens by giving the characters the time and the space they need to

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:17.980
live

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:18.940
breathe

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:19.740
and grow.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:35.180
The creators of the show don't tell us to care about Jimmy McGill

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:38.420
or to start to sympathize with Mike Ehrmantraut

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.980
They allow us to see them in action, good and bad

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.500
and we can't help but become endeared to them in the process.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.780
But it's not just slow; we also get, when appropriate

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:59.360
excellent use of montages to move us through time quickly.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:10.700
[smooth af blues]

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.280
Shows with rich, fully fleshed-out characters are more common in TV now

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.640
but it's still difficult to pull off.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.360
Better Call Saul has very human characters that do things that make sense for them to do

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.760
not just what the plot needs next.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.320
Jimmy's relationship with his brother Chuck is an excellent example of this.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:37.820
[Chuck] You look piqued

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.100
Jimmy: I'm fine, please just sit.

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.240
Chuck: You're not in trouble

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.400
While many viewers dislike Chuck

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.200
and tend to side with Jimmy as the protagonist of the show when conflict between them arises

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:52.360
Chuck is treated fairly sympathetically by the show.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:54.460
Chuck: I understand that you have... [stammering]...

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.680
great affection for Jimmy

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:57.240
a great many people do

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:58.860
but PLEASE open your eyes

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:05.040
We can understand his motivations and why he feels the way he does when conflict arises with his brother

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.920
and this is true to life.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.220
In life when a rift forms between two people

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.820
it can often be hard to place the blame on one of them in particular.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:14.820
Instead

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.580
there often tends to be parts of their personalities

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.360
or value systems that are at odds

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:22.300
and the blame is often a matter of perspective.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.300
The show doesn't paint its characters in black and white.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:26.760
Howard: Give Chuck my love

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:27.900
would you

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.340
It's hard to identify a real villain for the show.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.820
The show often lets us see and consider both sides of an issue

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:37.760
Kim: [clobbers Jimmy]

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:39.580
Jimmy: Kim... What?!

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:44.280
Kim: Just drive.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:45.460
Similarly

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:49.840
Kim and Jimmy's relationship is one that is given very little exposition.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.480
We don't know much about their history together

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:54.160
or even their current status

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.820
but we don't need to

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.760
because we're shown as much as we need to know to care about them.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:05.160
Jimmy: Yes Mrs Kettleman, so good of you to return...

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.240
a..actually I don't have Mr McGill at the moment, but I know he..

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.080
What is Better Call Saul?

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:11.380
A drama?

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:12.580
A comedy?

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:13.840
A crime show?

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.300
A courtroom drama?

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:16.500
A thriller?

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.280
Better Call Saul often seems to defy definition

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.360
existing outside any clear genre label.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.500
It is what it needs to be for any given episode or story beat.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.980
Some of the comedic scenes with Jimmy and the brilliant silent investigative scenes with Mike Ehrmantraut

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.440
feel like they could come from two different kinds of shows.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.220
But the scenes never feel dissonant

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:44.720
the comedy never feels out of place and the drama doesn't feel too somber.

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:50.500
It's a balancing act propelled by logical writing that serves the story instead of the genre.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.920
You never get the feeling that they added plot elements to keep it a crime show or a comedy

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.940
Better Call Saul is in a very unique position.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:08.300
We know how it ends before we even start the show.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:12.800
Sure we don't know the details and there can and will be some twists along the way

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:14.600
but from the very start

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.900
the audience knows what Jimmy will become.

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.600
They know who Saul Goodman is from Breaking Bad.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.900
But even if they don't, the cold-open flash-forwards from the start of each season

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.040
reminds us from the very beginning what the end game is for Jimmy McGill

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.620
Saul Goodman is an inevitable future for Jimmy McGill.

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:34.500
Hi I'm Saul Goodman

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.460
and I will do the fighting for you!

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:38.700
No charge is too big for me.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.800
when legal forces have you cornered

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:41.900
Better Call Saul!

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.340
How many shows announced the outcome in the very title?

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.660
Most show titles are a mere hint at the premise

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.100
but Better Call Saul is a thematically purposeful show;

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.160
it doesn't beat around the bush.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:00.120
I don't think that all shows should be more upfront and direct about their themes and where they're going

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.740
but the fact that Better Call Saul does this

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:06.100
sets it firmly apart in a sea of well-produced TV

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:10.360
It flirts with fatalism in a way that we rarely experience

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:15.720
on the small screen. In this way it's the perfect spinoff and development of Breaking Bad,

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:20.420
It doesn't just happen in the same story universe, it's cut from the same thematic cloth.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:23.660
Better Call Saul reverse-engineers Breaking Bad.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.320
Where Breaking Bad was about taking you on a journey of learning to hate a character

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.140
as they become completely evil

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.620
Better Call Saul is about showing you the past of someone

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.620
we already know will become a sleazy criminal

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:39.220
and making us feel sympathetic for that character

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:42.560
by showing us what made him become Saul Goodman.

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.560
Better Call Saul keeps the viewer in suspense

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.360
and I'm not talking about edge-of-your-seat

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.960
tension or fear that someone is going to jump out from behind a dumpster.

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:56.840
The writers are pulling you along with a series of overlapping questions and answers.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:02.700
I think this is a big way they're able to keep the show interesting even though the biggest dramatic question of the show has already

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.100
kind of been answered.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.080
By moving the audience's minds off of the question of what happens to Jimmy in the end,

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.960
the show lets them focus on how Jimmy gets there.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.260
The writers are comfortable with you as a viewer

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.740
being in the dark about something for entire scenes or episodes

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:22.760
and even when the answer is revealed

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:25.720
it doesn't feel like they have to spell it out for you.

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:28.520
Answers are slowly unraveled

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.740
like the true nature of Chuck's condition

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.380
and it creates an air of mystery that doesn't exist even in many

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:38.360
mystery and crime-solving shows on TV today.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.920
The Creators truly understand "show don't tell"

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:48.000
and watching Better Call Saul feels more like a discovery than being told a story

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:52.540
which for me is a cornerstone of a great experience with TV or film.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:53.860
Here's an example

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:59.460
In this scene Chuck shows Jimmy how to peel tape off of his wall so that the wood isn't damaged.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:07.620
Jimmy would rather rip it off quickly but Chuck is careful, methodical and precise, highlighting the differences between the two brothers

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:09.439
Nevertheless

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.380
Jimmy is willing to learn Chuck's method and apply it

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.580
A whole episode later, we get this scene

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:30.120
and with no dialogue so much is communicated about Jimmy's unconscious desire to fit into his brother's mold

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:33.480
and his conscious rebellion against that.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:46.160
Better Call Saul is endlessly drawing me in, making me think "what's going on inside that character's head?"

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:50.500
It's hard to rank Better Call Saul, among the many great TV shows right now

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.780
but I know it's one of the best. It's consistently fresh, interesting, logical

00:13:55.780 --> 00:14:01.100
and with great cinematography, music, rich characters, interesting themes and good writing

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.040
I find myself looking forward to where the show will lead

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.680
instead of as I do with most shows

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:10.020
just hoping the next season isn't worse than the one before.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.460
TV is an incredible feat;

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.140
it's hard to maintain quality

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:17.020
for 40 to 60 or even 100 hours

00:14:17.300 --> 00:14:19.120
so when a team is able to pull that off

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.620
I can't help but celebrate it.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:28.140
I'm Thomas Flight

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.480
